# Reflection

<details style="margin-top: 12px; border-radius: 8px; overflow: hidden;">
  <summary style="background-color: #E4E2F8; padding: 12px 16px; cursor: pointer; font-weight: bold; color: #1F1976; font-size: 16px;">
    &#128161; How does Lesson 11's "the most accurate model isn't always the right one" connect to Lesson 12's "someone has to be accountable for the model you choose"?
  </summary>
  <div style="background-color: #FFFFFF; padding: 16px; border: 1px solid #ACA8EA; font-size: 16px; color: #1A1A1A; line-height: 1.6;">
    Both point to the same idea: choosing a model is a human decision, not a math result. In Lesson 11, the model with the lowest error was sometimes the wrong one to use because of who it could harm. In Lesson 12, two students could build different "best" models depending on what they valued, like energy or fewer allergens. In both cases, the numbers can't make the call for you, which is exactly why a person has to be able to explain the choice and answer for it.
  </div>
</details>

<hr>

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-radius: 10px; padding: 18px 20px; margin: 24px 0 16px 0;">
